Q: Should RVO data use std::move to method that internally uses std::vector::emplace_back? A: <p><code>emplace_back</code> allows you to create the object in place. Hence, there is no need to construct the object before. Otherwise you could as well use <code>push_back</code> (it has a <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> overload as well: <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/vector/push_back" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/container/vector/push_back</a>).</p>
<p>To construct the element in place in the vector, pass the constructor arguments to <code>addData</code>:</p>
<pre><code>class Manager {
public:    
    void addData(auto&amp;&amp;... args) {
        m_vec.emplace_back(std::forward&lt;decltype(args)&gt;(args)...);
    }

private:
    std::vector&lt;Data&gt; m_vec;
};
</code></pre>
<p>As mentioned in a comment, the <code>std::move</code> is redundant here:</p>
<pre><code>manager.addData(std::move(dataMaker(789)));   
manager.addData(dataMaker(700));               // exactly the same
</code></pre>
<p>If you can emplace the element go for that. If you need to construct the object first and then insert it in the vector, either of the two lines in question are fine (but then the <code>emplace_back</code> is pointless and i'd write <code>push_back(a_data)</code> instead; and you have to pay a move, which is often less expensive than a copy, but also not for free).</p>
